-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Logarithm Domain:** The exit opacity $\mathbf{I}_\alpha(\mathbf{p})$ must be strictly between 0 and 1 for the log terms to be defined. In practice, values exactly at 0 or 1 will cause NaN gradients. Clamping to $[\epsilon, 1-\epsilon]$ is required [G].
-*   **Weight Sensitivity:** If $\lambda_B$ is too small, materials remain transparent and noisy. If too large, it may over-constrain the optimization, potentially distorting geometry or causing convergence issues [S1].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Singularities:** The Beta(0.5, 0.5) distribution has infinite density at 0 and 1. In practice, $\log(0)$ is undefined. Implementations must clamp opacity values away from exact 0 and 1 before taking the logarithm to ensure numerical stability [G].
+*   **Weight Sensitivity:** The optimal weight $\lambda_B$ is highly data-dependent. Too high a weight may oversimplify the geometry, while too low a weight fails to suppress noise [S1].
+*   **Material Loss:** While the Beta prior is robust, it is distinct from the Entropy prior. The Entropy prior can cause thin materials to vanish entirely, whereas the Beta prior tends to preserve material solidity [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Entropy Prior
-Uses an entropy-based penalty on exit opacities. It also encourages binary opacities but tends to lose thin materials (e.g., hair tails) compared to the Beta prior [S1]. See [Entropy Prior](link).
+Uses an entropy-based regularization to encourage binary opacity. It tends to lose thin materials (e.g., flow tails) more aggressively than the Beta prior. See [Entropy Prior](2962-entropy-prior.md).
 
 ### Dream Field Prior
-Applies a prior on mean transmittance. Changes are less clear visually compared to Beta or Entropy priors [S1]. See [Dream Field Prior](link).
+Applies a prior on the mean transmittance. Changes in visual quality are less distinct compared to Beta or Entropy priors. See [Dream Field Prior](2963-dream-field-prior.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(P)$, where $P$ is the number of pixels, as it requires a single pass over the rendered opacity image.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(P)$ to store the exit opacity image. No additional large buffers are needed.
-*   **Strengths:** Consistently produces images with more solid material and less noise compared to training without a prior or using the Entropy prior [S1]. Effective at reducing "floaters" and semi-transparent artifacts.
-*   **Limitations:** The optimal weight $\lambda_B$ is highly data-dependent and requires manual tuning or experimentation [S1]. May cause loss of delicate details if the weight is too high.
-*   **When to Use:** When the rendered volume appears too transparent, noisy, or contains significant floaters, and when preserving thin structures is less critical than achieving solid, coherent material appearance.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the scene contains very thin, semi-transparent structures that should be preserved (e.g., smoke, hair) and the Entropy prior is insufficient, as the Beta prior may also suppress these features if not carefully tuned.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(P)$, where $P$ is the number of pixels, as it requires a single pass over the rendered opacity map.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(P)$ to store the intermediate log terms if not computed in-place.
+*   **Strengths:** Consistently produces solid materials with less noise compared to training without a prior [S1]. It is more stable than the Entropy prior regarding material preservation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the weight $\lambda_B$ per dataset [S1]. The singularities of the Beta distribution require numerical clamping [G].
+*   **When to Use:** When the rendered volume exhibits excessive transparency, floaters, or noise, and when preserving thin but important material structures is critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the scene naturally contains significant semi-transparent regions that should not be forced to binary opacity, or if computational budget is extremely tight (though the cost is low).
 
 ## Background
-The Beta Distribution Prior was introduced by Lombardi et al. in the context of neural volume rendering [S1, S2]. It was one of the first density priors applied to this domain, followed by the Dream Field prior (prior on mean transmittance) and the Entropy prior [S1]. The method is based on the intuition that rays should primarily strike the object or the background, rather than terminating in mid-air, thus reducing the entropy of the exit opacity distribution [S2].
+The Beta Distribution Prior was introduced by Lombardi et al. in the context of Neural Volumes [S2]. It was one of the first density priors applied to neural volume rendering, followed by the Dream Field prior (prior on mean transmittance) and the Entropy prior [S1]. The method is based on the intuition that rays in a well-posed volume rendering problem should either strike the object or the background, rather than grazing surfaces with intermediate opacities [S2].
 
